            <ArticleTitle>[Myocardial bridging, or milking, as a cause of chest pain: report of 4 cases].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>117-120</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="EN" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Myocardial bridging of epicardial coronary arteries can cause a compression effect during systole. The anterior descending artery is the most commonly affected. This phenomenon is also known as milking. The clinical spectrum is varied. The patient may be symptom-free or experience varying degrees of chest pain during exercise or rest. We describe 4 patients treated for myocardial bridging in a hospital emergency department. Physicians should become familiar with this phenomenon in order to understand its management and implications.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
